   "text": "The storm had been building all afternoon, and by the time Mara reached the lighthouse, the sky was the color of a bruise. She pushed the heavy door open with her shoulder and stepped inside, shaking the rain from her coat. The keeper, an old man named Tobias, looked up from his chair by the stove and raised an eyebrow. \"You're either very brave or very lost,\" he said. Mara laughed, a little too loudly, and admitted that it was probably a bit of both. She had driven three hours along the coast road, following a letter that had arrived at her apartment with no return address. Inside the envelope was a single photograph of this lighthouse, and on the back, in her grandmother's handwriting, were four words: come before the winter. Tobias studied the photograph for a long time. Then he stood, slowly, and walked to a wooden cabinet against the far wall. He unlocked it with a brass key and took out a small tin box, dented and rusted at the corners. \"Your grandmother left this with me forty years ago,\" he said quietly. \"She told me someone would come for it one day. I stopped believing her a long time ago.\" Mara held the box in both hands. It was lighter than she expected. Outside, the wind howled against the glass, and somewhere far below, the waves crashed against the rocks. She opened the lid. Inside were dozens of letters, tied together with a faded blue ribbon, and a map drawn by hand on yellowed paper. At the center of the map was a small island she had never heard of, circled twice in red ink. Mara looked up at Tobias, and for the first time that night, he smiled. \"Well,\" he said, \"I suppose you'll be needing a boat.\"",
